## Introduction

A core idea in signal processing is that signals can be represented as combinations of oscillations.

Periodic signals can be expressed as sums of sinusoids using Fourier series. More general, nonperiodic signals can be described as a continuous mixture of complex exponentials using the Fourier transform.

In practice, we work with sampled data of finite length. In this setting, the Fourier transform becomes the discrete Fourier transform (DFT). The DFT represents any finite sequence such as audio, images, or time-series data as a weighted sum of discrete frequencies.

From a linear algebra perspective, this process is a change of basis.

